'''baseness''' (bās′nes), ''n.'' [< ''base''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ness.''] '''1.''' The state or condition of being base or low in scale; meanness of grade; lowliness, as of birth or station.
{{smaller block|''Baseness'' of birth is a great disparagement to some men.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 347.}}
<poem>He mixing with his proper sphere,
She finds the ''baseness'' of her lot.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, lx.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is base or low; anything of an ignoble grade or quality; meanness, as of relation or employment.
{{smaller block|<poem>Some kinds of ''baseness''
Are nobly undergone. {{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, iii. 1.}}
I once did hold it a ''baseness'' to write fair.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 2.}}}}
'''3†.''' Illegitimacy of birth; bastardy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Why brand they us
With base? with ''baseness'', bastardy?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 2.}}}}
'''4.''' The state or quality of being morally mean or vile; vileness; worthlessness.
{{smaller block|<poem>Villains,
Whose ''baseness'' all disgraceful words made one
Cannot express!</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Malta, iv. 4.}}
<poem>Equal ''baseness'' lived in sleeker times.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, v.}}}}
'''5.''' Of metals: (''a'') Liability to rust: opposed to ''nobleness.'' (''b'') Inferior or debased quality, the result of having been alloyed with a cheaper metal; spuriousness.

